---
import { LISTED_PLAN_IDS, PLANS } from "@emojisense/platform";
import Callout from "../../components/docs/Callout.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/docs/CodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/docs/DocsLayout.astro";
import { API_HOST } from "../../components/docs/facts";
import { DASHBOARD_URL } from "../../config";
import { formatCount } from "../../lib/format";

const overLimit = `{
  "query": "…",
  "results": [/* dictionary results only (hybrid), or none (semantic) */],
  "cached": false,
  "degraded": false,
  "overLimit": true
}`;

const publishable = `fetch("${API_HOST}/v1/search?q=ship%20it&key=pk_live_…");`;
const secret = `curl "${API_HOST}/v1/search?q=ship%20it" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer sk_live_…"`;
---

<DocsLayout path="/docs/keys-and-limits/">
  <p>Keys tell the API which app is calling, so it can apply that app’s plan and rate limits. There are two kinds, one for code that runs in front of people and one for servers.</p>

  <h2 id="key-types">Publishable and secret keys</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Key</th><th scope="col">Use in</th><th scope="col">Sent as</th><th scope="col">Checks</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Publishable <code>pk_live_…</code></td><td>Browsers and extensions</td><td><code>?key=</code> in the URL</td><td>The <code>Origin</code> must be one of the key’s allowed origins</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Secret <code>sk_live_…</code></td><td>Servers, bots, MCP, tenant management</td><td><code>Authorization: Bearer</code></td><td>Refused with an <code>Origin</code> header or in the URL</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock code={publishable} lang="ts" title="Publishable key" />
  <CodeBlock code={secret} lang="bash" title="Secret key" />
  <p>Create keys in the <a href={`${DASHBOARD_URL}/`}>dashboard</a>, per app and environment: production on every plan, development from Solo and staging from Pro. The full key is shown once. The dashboard stores only a hash of it. Call the API at <code>{API_HOST}</code> over HTTPS: a plain <code>http://</code> request gets <code>403</code>, so a key never travels unencrypted.</p>
  <Callout type="warning" title="Origins are a browser control">
    <p>The <code>Origin</code> check stops other websites from using your publishable key. It cannot stop a server, which can send any header, so every key also has a rate limit. Never put a secret key in a browser, an app bundle or a repository.</p>
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="origins">Allowed origins</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Add every origin your app runs on, for example <code>https://your.app</code> and <code>http://localhost:5173</code>.</li>
    <li>An empty list allows any origin. Only <code>dev</code> keys may have one, for localhost and preview builds.</li>
    <li>For a Chrome extension, add the extension origin shown on its options page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="revoking">Revoking</h2>
  <p>Revoke a key in the dashboard. The API caches key lookups for up to 60 seconds, so a revoked key stops working within a minute. An unknown or revoked key gets <code>401</code>.</p>

  <h2 id="rate-limits">Rate limits</h2>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Caller</th><th scope="col">Limit</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>A key</td><td>120 requests a minute, per key and IP address</td></tr>
        <tr><td>No key</td><td>30 requests a minute, per IP address</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Over it, the API answers <code>429</code> with <code>Retry-After: 60</code>. The IP address is only an in-memory key for the limiter. It is never logged or stored. Static files and emoji images are not limited.</p>

  <h2 id="without-a-key">Without a key</h2>
  <p>Search, reaction suggestions and photo to emoji also work without a key, for demos and first tests. Such calls are never metered and never over a limit, but they get the stricter rate limit, no custom emoji and no analytics. Custom packs and the tenants API need a key.</p>

  <h2 id="plan-limits">Plan limits</h2>
  <p>Limits are monthly, in UTC, with no daily caps. Only AI calls are metered on the plans. An AI call is a search or a reaction suggestion that reaches the API, a cached answer included. On-device searches, packs and shards are free and unlimited on every plan.</p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Plan</th>
          <th scope="col">Price</th>
          <th scope="col">AI calls</th>
          <th scope="col">Apps</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          LISTED_PLAN_IDS.map((id) => (
            <tr>
              <td>{PLANS[id].name}</td>
              <td>{PLANS[id].priceUsdMonthly === 0 ? "Free" : `$${PLANS[id].priceUsdMonthly} a month`}</td>
              <td>{formatCount(PLANS[id].limits.semantic_calls)}</td>
              <td>{formatCount(PLANS[id].maxApps)}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>See <a href="/pricing/">Pricing</a> for every feature of each plan. Buy a paid plan on the dashboard's Billing page; the new limits apply to every app of the account within a minute. Limits belong to the account, so the calls of all its apps count together. The dashboard shows each app’s part of the month’s usage.</p>

  <h2 id="over-the-limit">Over the limit</h2>
  <p>The API never fails because of a plan limit. A query that is in your account’s edge cache is still answered, and is not counted. Any other query returns <code>200</code> with <code>"overLimit": true</code>:</p>
  <CodeBlock code={overLimit} lang="json" />
  <p>The SDKs handle this for you: search stays on the device and the shards, and people keep typing as before. Photo classification returns no results until the next month.</p>
</DocsLayout>
